\title[GuardShield-Runtime]{GuardShield-Runtime: Artifact-Aware Runtime Feedback for Progress-Preserving Safe Reinforcement Learning in Dense Traffic}

\author*[1]{\fnm{Anonymous} \sur{Author}}\email{anonymous@example.com}
\affil*[1]{\orgdiv{Department}, \orgname{Institution}, \orgaddress{\city{City}, \country{Country}}}

\abstract{Safe reinforcement learning in dense traffic can produce misleading safety evidence when a policy lowers measured cost by suppressing motion rather than by regulating risk while maintaining route progress. We treat this failure as a closed-loop feedback artifact and propose GuardShield-Runtime, a simulator-level framework that couples a learned driving policy with runtime risk monitoring, bounded action intervention, and progress-safety diagnostics. Before execution, the framework monitors time-to-collision, speed, route progress, and lane-related signals to classify local risk, then bounds risk-triggered acceleration commands while logging each intervention. In MetaDrive dense-traffic simulations at density 0.15, the Risk-only baseline achieves 0.0\% cost, but this apparent safety is accompanied by a 100.0\% stop ratio and only 0.9\% route completion. In contrast, the runtime-intervention variants preserve 85.7--86.9\% route completion with 18.3--20.8\% cost. A clean same-checkpoint ablation further shows that combining TTC and speed feedback reduces cost from 100.0\% to 20.8\% while raising route completion from 21.8\% to 85.3\%, whereas TTC-only and speed-only interventions remain less effective. These results suggest that dense-traffic safe reinforcement learning should be evaluated as monitored feedback regulation, where collision cost, route progress, stopping behavior, and intervention burden are interpreted jointly.}

\keywords{safe reinforcement learning, runtime feedback intervention, artifact-aware evaluation, autonomous driving simulation, cybernetic control}

