\section{Introduction}\label{sec_introduction}

Dense autonomous-driving control is inherently a closed-loop system--environment interaction problem. A policy issues an action, surrounding traffic responds, the local state evolves, and the next action is selected under a changed risk condition. This feedback structure is central to cybernetics and control theory \citep{wiener1948cybernetics,astrom2008feedback}, and it also creates the setting in which reinforcement-learning policies can produce misleading safety evidence. In dense simulated traffic, low measured cost can arise either because the ego vehicle regulates risk while making progress or because it stops and avoids interaction altogether. From a cybernetic perspective, the artifact arises because the learned controller can alter the system--environment interaction mode itself: from risk-regulated driving to non-interactive stopping.

This distinction matters because measured cost is often treated as evidence of safety in safe reinforcement learning. Standard deep reinforcement learning optimizes long-horizon return, while safe-RL methods augment this objective with cost constraints, penalties, or risk-sensitive terms \citep{altman1999constrained,garcia2015safe,achiam2017constrained,chow2018lyapunov}. However, when safety is evaluated primarily through cost, a policy can exploit the objective by suppressing motion. In driving, this behavior should not be interpreted as safe task completion; it is task avoidance. Route progress, stop ratio, low-progress rate, time-to-collision (TTC) danger, and intervention frequency must therefore be measured alongside collision-oriented cost.

Runtime feedback intervention provides a complementary mechanism for addressing this failure. We do not treat a TTC rule or a speed cap as a final safety controller. Instead, we use them as auditable runtime feedback actuators for testing whether a safe-RL objective produces progress-preserving safety or merely lets the vehicle stop to avoid measured risk. This creates a monitored closed loop in which the policy proposes actions, the monitor estimates local risk, the intervention operator bounds risk-triggered acceleration commands, and the environment returns the next observation. The resulting loop can then be audited through progress-safety diagnostics.

To operationalize this idea, we introduce GuardShield-Runtime. The framework includes runtime-intervention variants that separate execution-side action bounding from reward-side risk shaping, allowing the diagnostic protocol to distinguish stationary reward exploitation from progress-preserving feedback regulation. We compare against artifact controls, including Risk-only and No-action guard, and external baselines, including PPO-Lagrangian and a TTC/RSS-style runtime filter. The claim is intentionally limited: GuardShield-Runtime is a simulator-level runtime feedback and diagnostic framework, not a proof-producing safety shield or a vehicle-grade real-time controller.

This paper makes four contributions. First, it formulates and empirically exposes the stationary safety artifact: cost-oriented safe-RL evaluation can be misleading when a policy suppresses motion instead of regulating risk while completing the route. Second, it proposes GuardShield-Runtime as a runtime feedback architecture that combines a PPO driving policy, TTC/speed/progress/lane monitoring, bounded longitudinal intervention, and auditable intervention logging. Third, it provides a clean mechanism test through same-checkpoint runtime ablation, showing that TTC-only and speed-only feedback are insufficient and that the combined operator supplies the main execution-side improvement. Fourth, it packages artifact-aware evaluation around density stress, sensitivity, uncertainty, intervention burden, loop timing, and reproducibility artifacts so that collision cost, route progress, stopping behavior, TTC danger, and intervention frequency are interpreted together.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec_related_work}

\subsection{Safe reinforcement learning and constrained policy optimization}
Safe reinforcement learning seeks to optimize task reward while constraining or penalizing unsafe outcomes such as collisions, constraint violations, or unsafe exploration \citep{garcia2015safe,amodei2016concrete,leike2017ai}. Constrained Markov decision processes, Constrained Policy Optimization, and related Lagrangian approaches formalize this idea by treating safety cost as a constraint or penalty during learning \citep{altman1999constrained,achiam2017constrained,tessler2018reward}. Recent infrastructure work further emphasizes the need for reproducible safe-RL benchmark implementations \citep{ji2024omnisafe}. Other approaches use Lyapunov arguments, model-based stability conditions, or safety critics to guide or restrict policy behavior \citep{berkenkamp2017safe,chow2018lyapunov,dalal2018safe}. These methods are important, but their simulator evidence can remain ambiguous in closed-loop driving when low cost is achieved through motion suppression rather than risk-regulated progress. The non-motion artifact can be viewed as a driving-specific instance of reward hacking or specification gaming, where optimizing a proxy objective yields behavior that satisfies the measured signal without satisfying the intended task \citep{amodei2016concrete,pan2022reward,skalse2022defining}. Our study therefore treats cost as one diagnostic signal among route completion, stop ratio, TTC danger, and intervention burden, rather than as the sole definition of safe driving.

\subsection{Runtime shielding, safety filters, and vehicle rules}
Runtime shielding modifies actions online when a monitored condition is violated \citep{alshiekh2018shielding,saunders2018trial}. This idea is also related to runtime-assurance and Simplex-style architectures, where an advanced controller is supervised by a simpler fallback or safety controller \citep{seto1998simplex}. Recent runtime-assurance work similarly studies how to use learning to manage fallback-controller utilization while maintaining hard constraints \citep{miller2024optimal}. In driving, rule-based safety models such as RSS provide interpretable distance or TTC-style constraints \citep{shalev2017rss}. Control barrier functions and reachability methods provide more formal languages for filtering or constraining actions \citep{ames2019control,fisac2019bridging}. GuardShield-Runtime belongs to this runtime-intervention family, but it addresses a different evidential question: whether action filtering reduces measured cost while preserving route progress, or merely hides a failure mode. Accordingly, we do not claim a formal invariant set or certified safe controller; instead, we study measurable runtime feedback intervention in a closed-loop simulator.

\subsection{Cybernetic feedback and system--environment interaction}
Cybernetics emphasizes closed-loop regulation through sensing, feedback, and action \citep{wiener1948cybernetics,astrom2008feedback}. Reinforcement learning also operates through an action--environment--observation loop, but the optimized objective need not align with the desired closed-loop behavior. In our setting, the runtime monitor acts as a feedback sensor, the intervention operator acts as a bounded actuator, the learned policy plus runtime operator forms the controller, and progress-safety metrics serve as diagnostic outputs. This framing is useful because it makes the central failure observable and measurable: a policy may reduce cost by changing the closed-loop interaction from driving to stationary avoidance. GuardShield-Runtime uses this perspective to evaluate the learned policy not as an isolated function approximator, but as one component of a monitored feedback system.

\subsection{Closed-loop autonomous-driving simulation}
Simulation platforms support repeatable evaluation of policy learning, planning, and control, and autonomous-driving simulators such as CARLA and MetaDrive provide closed-loop traffic scenarios for testing learned driving policies \citep{dosovitskiy2017carla,li2022metadrive}. Deep RL has been explored for autonomous-driving control and decision making \citep{sallab2017deep,kendall2019learning,grigorescu2020survey,kiran2021deep,kuutti2021survey}. However, closed-loop evaluation remains sensitive to scenario design, reward scale, baseline choice, random seed, and safety-case interpretation \citep{koopman2017autonomous,henderson2018deep}. Recent benchmark suites such as Safety-Gymnasium and SafeBench further show the need for standardized safety tasks, scenario coverage, and diagnostic metrics beyond a single cost scalar \citep{ji2023safetygymnasium,xu2022safebench}. Recent autonomous-driving evaluation work also emphasizes closed-loop assessment, scenario coverage, and safety-relevant evaluation rather than relying only on open-loop or aggregate metrics \citep{feng2023dense,jia2024bench2drive}. MetaDrive is useful for this study because it provides compositional traffic scenarios together with route-completion diagnostics, making progress collapse visible under dense interaction \citep{li2022metadrive}. We therefore use MetaDrive as a controlled diagnostic platform for testing whether runtime intervention reduces cost while preserving route progress under dense closed-loop interaction.

\subsection{Positioning of this work}
GuardShield-Runtime is positioned between training-time safe RL, runtime filtering, and diagnostic evaluation. Unlike reward-only risk shaping, it modifies the action before execution and logs each intervention. Unlike RSS or CBF-style formal filters, it is not presented as a proof of safety, but as a way to audit whether lower cost coincides with useful driving behavior. Its role is to test and document, through diagnostics, whether runtime feedback intervention can mitigate a non-motion artifact that cost-only safe-RL evaluation would otherwise hide. The contribution is therefore not a proof-producing safety method, but a diagnostic feedback architecture that makes proxy-objective failure observable under closed-loop driving evaluation. The closest prior mechanisms are TTC/RSS-style runtime filters, shielding and runtime-assurance architectures, and reward-misspecification analyses. The present distinction is the combined focus on action-side feedback, motion-gated risk shaping, and artifact-aware progress diagnostics under the same simulator protocol.

