\subsection{Problem setting and closed-loop interface}\label{sec_problem_signals}
Let $o_t$ denote the simulator observation and let the learned policy propose a two-dimensional continuous action $a_t^\pi=(\delta_t,u_t^\pi)$, where $\delta_t$ denotes steering and $u_t^\pi$ denotes the normalized acceleration/braking command. GuardShield-Runtime does not replace the learned policy; it inserts an intervention operator $\mathcal{I}_\theta$ between the policy output and the simulator execution interface:
\begin{equation}
a_t^{\mathrm{exec}}=\mathcal{I}_\theta(o_t,a_t^\pi),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
o_{t+1}\sim P(\cdot\mid o_t,a_t^{\mathrm{exec}}).
\end{equation}
The runtime layer uses observable wrapper-level signals available during closed-loop simulation: ego speed $v_t$, lane deviation $d_t^{\mathrm{lane}}$, route-progress increment $\Delta p_t$, forward time-to-collision $\mathrm{TTC}_t$, intervention events, and wall-clock loop timing. Table~\ref{tab_runtime_signals} defines how these signals enter the runtime monitor and how they are logged for diagnostics. This design keeps the intervention and diagnostics auditable at the wrapper level, rather than relying on post-hoc episode labels alone.

