\subsection{Failure mode: stationary safety artifact}\label{sec_nonmotion_method}
The key failure mode is what we call stationary safety: the policy appears safe under collision-oriented cost because it avoids motion. If a risk penalty or cost objective primarily rewards the absence of collision without sufficiently requiring route progress, the learned policy can reduce measured cost by slowing or stopping. This is not merely a reporting inconvenience: it changes the interpretation of a safe-RL result. A method with low cost, near-zero route completion, and high stop ratio should not be interpreted as progress-preserving safe driving. For this reason, every main result reports cost together with success, route completion, low-progress rate, stop ratio, TTC-danger fraction, and intervention frequency.

\subsection{Learning backbone and reward variants}\label{sec_learning_backbone}
The learned backbone is a PPO policy $\pi_\phi(o_t)$ \citep{schulman2017ppo} trained on the native MetaDrive observation space with a two-dimensional continuous action for steering and longitudinal control. MetaDrive supplies the base task reward and termination signals; the risk-aware wrappers modify the step reward according to the variant configuration. When risk reward is enabled, the shaped objective combines scaled task reward with penalties for nonzero simulator cost, TTC risk, lane deviation, action variation, and overspeed. It also includes route-progress reward, idle penalty, success bonus, crash penalty, and out-of-road penalty according to the variant configuration, with detailed coefficients reported in Appendix~\ref{app_manifest}.

The Risk-only and No-action-guard artifact-control variants keep the risk-oriented reward but disable execution-side action modification. Guard and Shield use the same TTC-plus-speed runtime operator without continuous risk reward; they differ only in training context, with Guard trained under the curriculum schedule and Shield trained without curriculum. Gated-risk uses the same runtime operator and activates the continuous risk penalty only when the ego vehicle is in an active-control state and a monitored risk signal is present:
\begin{equation}
m_t=\mathbf{1}\left[v_t\geq 3\ \mathrm{km/h}\ \lor\ \Delta p_t>10^{-4}\right],
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
q_t=\mathbf{1}\left[\rho_t^{\mathrm{TTC}}\geq0.01\ \lor\ d_t^{\mathrm{lane}}\geq0.25\ \lor\ \rho_t^{\mathrm{speed}}>0\right],
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
g_t=m_t q_t.
\end{equation}
Here, $\rho_t^{\mathrm{TTC}}$ denotes the wrapper-computed TTC-risk score and $\rho_t^{\mathrm{speed}}=\max(v_t-v^\star,0)/\max(v^\star,10^{-6})$ denotes the overspeed signal relative to the target-speed band. Thus $c_t^{\mathrm{risk}}$ is used only through the gate $g_t$, while the simulator event cost remains logged separately as the binary episode-level safety outcome. The curriculum changes only the training density, map complexity, traffic/event probability schedule, and horizon schedule; it does not change the runtime intervention operator used in diagnostic evaluation.

\subsection{Runtime monitor}\label{sec_ttc_monitor}
The runtime monitor estimates a forward TTC signal from nearby vehicles that are ahead of the ego vehicle and closing in relative speed. Let $\mathcal{F}_t$ denote the monitored set of forward vehicles with positive longitudinal distance, acceptable lateral offset, and positive relative closing speed. For vehicle $i\in\mathcal{F}_t$, let $d_i(t)$ be the forward distance and $v_{\mathrm{ego}}(t)-v_i(t)$ be the relative closing speed. The monitored TTC is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{TTC}_t=
\begin{cases}
\min_{i\in\mathcal{F}_t}\dfrac{d_i(t)}{v_{\mathrm{ego}}(t)-v_i(t)+\epsilon}, & \mathcal{F}_t\neq\emptyset,\\
+\infty, & \mathcal{F}_t=\emptyset.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The small $\epsilon$ prevents numerical instability. If no closing forward vehicle is available, the monitor assigns the clear TTC state rather than creating a spurious collision risk. The implementation also reports the continuous TTC-risk score $\rho_t^{\mathrm{TTC}}$, which increases as $\mathrm{TTC}_t$ falls below the stored TTC threshold. Hard, soft, and clear states are determined by $\tau_{\mathrm{hard}}$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{soft}}$; Section~\ref{sec_sensitivity} evaluates sensitivity to the TTC threshold independently from the core diagnostic run. A dangerous step is counted when the monitored TTC is below the diagnostic threshold used by the logger.

\subsection{Intervention operator}\label{sec_guard_operator}
In the reported implementation, the intervention operator acts on the longitudinal command and leaves the policy steering command unchanged. It first applies a TTC-based acceleration cap:
\begin{equation}
u_t^{\mathrm{guard}}=
\begin{cases}
\min(u_t^\pi,u_{\mathrm{hard}}), & \mathrm{TTC}_t<\tau_{\mathrm{hard}},\\
\min(u_t^\pi,u_{\mathrm{soft}}), & \tau_{\mathrm{hard}}\leq \mathrm{TTC}_t<\tau_{\mathrm{soft}},\\
u_t^\pi, & \mathrm{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $u_{\mathrm{hard}}=-1.0$, $u_{\mathrm{soft}}=-0.6$, and $\tau_{\mathrm{base}}$ is the stored TTC threshold. The hard and soft thresholds are $\tau_{\mathrm{hard}}=\max(3.5,0.60\tau_{\mathrm{base}})$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{soft}}=\max(6.0,\tau_{\mathrm{base}})$. A speed guard then limits acceleration when the ego speed is above the target-speed band. With target speed $v^\star$, the speed cap is
\begin{equation}
u^{\mathrm{speed}}(v_t)=
\begin{cases}
-0.35, & v_t>v^\star+5,\\
0, & v_t>v^\star,\\
+\infty, & \mathrm{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and the executed longitudinal command is
\begin{equation}
u_t^{\mathrm{exec}}=\min\{u_t^{\mathrm{guard}},u^{\mathrm{speed}}(v_t)\},\qquad
a_t^{\mathrm{exec}}=(\delta_t,u_t^{\mathrm{exec}}).
\end{equation}
The operator is per-step and memoryless in the reported implementation. It does not optimize a trajectory or certify future safety; it enforces a per-step command bound before the simulator step and logs whether $a_t^{\mathrm{exec}}\ne a_t^\pi$. Intervention frequency is therefore reported as interventions per 100 control steps. A high value is not automatically better or worse: it indicates how often the runtime layer overrides the proposed command under the monitored conditions.

\subsection{Diagnostic scope and relation to TTC/RSS filtering}\label{sec_not_only_ttc}
GuardShield-Runtime is close to TTC/RSS-style runtime filtering because both modify commands before environment execution. The distinction is diagnostic and architectural rather than certification-based. The external RSS/TTC comparator is a single rule-based runtime filter driven by distance or TTC thresholds. GuardShield-Runtime instead treats the intervention as one component of a closed-loop evidence system: TTC danger, stop ratio, low-progress episodes, route completion, intervention frequency, and loop timing are reported together so that lower cost cannot be mistaken for useful driving when progress collapses.

The Gated-risk variant also addresses a reward-side failure mode that a pure execution filter does not target. Its risk penalty is active only when speed or route progress indicates an active control state, which is intended to reduce stationary exploitation of a risk objective. This scope means that the TTC cap should not be read as a certified safety rule. Instead, the framework uses runtime action intervention together with artifact-aware diagnostics to test whether dense-traffic safe-RL evidence reflects progress-preserving feedback regulation or conservative execution filtering alone.

\subsection{Variant definitions}\label{sec_variants}
Table~\ref{tab_compared_methods} summarizes the compared variants by separating the policy backbone, reward-side risk shaping, action-side intervention, speed guard, and motion/progress gate. \textbf{PPO backbone} is the unmodified learned policy and serves as the lower-bound runtime comparator. \textbf{Risk-only} uses risk reward without action intervention and is included to test whether low cost can be achieved through stationary avoidance. \textbf{No-action guard} keeps the risk-oriented reward but disables execution-side modification, testing whether reward shaping alone can recover progress-preserving low-cost behavior. \textbf{Guard} and \textbf{Shield} apply the same TTC-plus-speed runtime operator without continuous risk reward, while \textbf{Gated-risk} combines runtime intervention with the motion/risk-gated reward shaping defined by $g_t$ above.

This gate is designed to reduce stationary exploitation of a continuous risk penalty: if the agent stops, the risk penalty is no longer allowed to masquerade as progress-preserving behavior. Guard and Shield share the same execution-side operator but differ in training context; they should therefore be read as deployment variants rather than a clean single-factor ablation. The clean same-checkpoint runtime-operator contrast is provided separately in the Results section.

