\section{Experimental Protocol}\label{sec_experimental_protocol}

\subsection{Environment and task}\label{sec_environment_task}
All experiments use MetaDrive as a controlled closed-loop dense-traffic driving simulator. The policy outputs continuous control commands and is evaluated using route progress, task success, collision or off-road cost, stop ratio, low-progress rate, speed, TTC-danger fraction, intervention frequency, and wall-clock simulation-loop timing. Success denotes the simulator episode-success flag for completing the driving task without terminal failure. Cost is reported as the percentage of episodes with a nonzero safety-cost event, which in this protocol is mainly caused by collision or out-of-road termination. Traffic density denotes the MetaDrive \(\texttt{traffic\_density}\) setting that controls background-traffic generation. The main diagnostic density is 0.15, and the density-stress study additionally evaluates 0.08, 0.20, and 0.25. Diagnostic episodes terminate on simulator success, simulator failure, or the fixed 1500-step horizon.

\subsection{Compared methods}\label{sec_compared_methods}
The compared rows are defined in Table~\ref{tab_compared_methods}. PPO backbone is the unmodified learned policy and serves as the lower-bound comparator for runtime intervention. Risk-only and No-action guard are artifact-control variants that test whether risk-oriented reward shaping alone can reduce cost without producing useful route progress. Guard, Shield, and Gated-risk are the proposed runtime-intervention variants. The PPO-Lagrangian row is a tested training-time constrained baseline under the same environment family and seed protocol; its zero-success result is reported as a protocol outcome, not as a universal claim about all constrained RL implementations. The RSS/TTC filter is a meaningful external runtime comparator because it also modifies behavior through an interpretable distance or TTC rule before execution.

\subsection{Training protocol and checkpoint selection}\label{sec_training_protocol}
Training uses 16 parallel environments. The reported PPO-family checkpoints are trained for one million timesteps per seed with horizon 1500 and \(\texttt{ent\_coef}=0.01\). Each density-0.15 aggregate reports three independently trained seeds. Unless otherwise stated, each seed contributes 40 diagnostic episodes, for 120 episodes per aggregate row. The default target speed for the proposed runtime variants is 18 km/h, and the stored reward/action-guard TTC threshold for the core configuration is 12.0 s. The external RSS/TTC comparator is parameterized separately with soft and hard TTC thresholds of 6.0 s and 3.5 s and a 20 km/h target speed. Checkpoints are fixed before diagnostic evaluation, and no episode-level post-hoc filtering is used to select favorable outcomes. Full PPO, PPO-Lagrangian, RSS/TTC, and runtime-variant hyperparameters are listed in Appendix~\ref{app_manifest}, with run-level configuration files retained in the supplementary package.

\subsection{Diagnostic evaluation protocol}\label{sec_eval_protocol}
Diagnostic evaluations are run as separate per-seed jobs so that episode-level TTC, speed, stop, intervention, route, and latency statistics remain traceable. Each density-0.15 aggregate row uses 120 diagnostic episodes across three training seeds, with 40 episodes per seed. Density stress applies the same diagnostic logic across four traffic densities. Sensitivity sweeps are independent diagnostic reruns; they should therefore be interpreted as trend evidence rather than exact numerical reproductions of the core default row. Appendix~\ref{app_manifest} reports the detailed parameter settings and result-block provenance.

\subsection{Statistical reporting}\label{sec_stats_protocol}
Main tables report one decimal place for percent metrics. Appendix B reports Wilson intervals for binary outcomes and seed-level mean/range summaries for the density-0.15 comparison; the full bootstrap and per-seed CSVs remain in the supplementary package. These intervals document uncertainty within the simulator diagnostic protocol. Because the study uses three independently trained seeds, we do not use the intervals to claim universal policy-family dominance or real-world validity.

\subsection{Supplementary artifacts}\label{sec_evidence_package}
The supplementary package includes anonymized episode-level results, aggregate tables, plotting scripts, table-generation scripts, and configuration files required to reproduce the reported figures and tables. The package is organized by evidence block, matching the core diagnostics, external baselines, density stress, sensitivity sweeps, runtime diagnostics, and uncertainty summaries reported in the manuscript.

\section{Results}\label{sec_results}

\subsection{Stationary safety artifact under cost-only evaluation}\label{sec_artifact}
Low measured cost does not necessarily indicate progress-preserving safe behavior. Figure~\ref{fig_core_evidence} and Table~\ref{tab_core_mechanism} show that Risk-only obtains 0.0\% cost at density 0.15, but this apparent safety is accompanied by 0.9\% route completion, 100.0\% stop ratio, and 100.0\% low-progress episodes. The PPO backbone shows the opposite failure mode: it continues to move, but it reaches 100.0\% cost with only 20.7\% route completion. The artifact is therefore a substantive evaluation failure rather than a reporting detail. It becomes visible only when motion and progress diagnostics are reported next to collision-oriented cost.

