The PPO backbone is not selected as an artificially weakened baseline. It is the fixed learned policy used by the runtime-filter rows and is reported as a lower-bound/backbone comparator. Its zero-success result reflects the dense diagnostic success criterion: the policy still moves, with 20.7\% route completion and 41.1 km/h mean speed at density 0.15, but frequent collision or out-of-road cost prevents successful completion. The same sanity check applies to PPO-Lagrangian, which reaches 22.1\% route completion and 39.4 km/h mean speed but terminates through collision and out-road events. Risk-only is different: it has no collision or out-road cost because it almost does not move. This sanity check motivates the inclusion of the external RSS/TTC filter as a runtime-filter comparator, rather than relying only on PPO-family training baselines.

\subsection{Same-checkpoint evidence for execution-side intervention}\label{sec_mechanism}
The improvement is not explained by reward shaping alone. No-action guard retains the risk-oriented objective but disables execution-side action intervention; it remains at 100.0\% cost. Guard, Shield, and Gated-risk instead reduce cost to 18.3--20.8\% while preserving 85.7--86.9\% route completion. This comparison suggests that reward shaping alone is insufficient to explain the improvement. Under this comparison, the key difference is whether the proposed longitudinal command is bounded before execution.

Table~\ref{tab_core_mechanism} establishes the artifact and the deployment-level effect of the runtime-intervention family. Table~\ref{tab_clean_runtime_ablation} is the primary mechanism evidence because it fixes the policy checkpoints and changes only the runtime operator. This removes the main training-context confound. TTC-only and speed-only interventions both improve the backbone, but neither single operator matches the combined intervention. The TTC cap reduces TTC-danger from 58.2\% to 10.5\%, but it retains 65.0\% cost and 56.3\% route completion. The speed guard raises route completion to 66.2\%, but it leaves TTC-danger at 32.7\% and cost at 65.0\%. Combining TTC and speed guards reduces cost to 20.8\%, preserves 85.3\% route completion, and reduces low-progress episodes to 1.7\%. The mechanism is therefore not explained by a single speed limit or a single TTC cap; under the same-checkpoint protocol, TTC and speed feedback are complementary.

\input{table_clean_runtime_ablation_pub.tex}

Because Guard, Shield, and Gated-risk are not strict single-policy ablations, we interpret them as a runtime-intervention family and rely on Table~\ref{tab_clean_runtime_ablation} for the clean operator contrast. The high intervention rate further shows that the learned backbone has not internalized the guard; execution-side feedback mitigates monitor-flagged proposals under the reported simulator protocol.

\subsection{Comparison with runtime and constrained-RL baselines}\label{sec_external}
Table~\ref{tab_external_baseline} shows that the TTC/RSS-style filter is a nontrivial runtime comparator: relative to the PPO backbone, it improves success to 53.3\% and reduces cost to 44.2\%. However, Guard, Shield, and Gated-risk reduce cost further, to 18.3--20.8\%, while preserving 85.7--86.9\% route completion. RSS/TTC also has a similar intervention frequency to the proposed variants, with 71.7 interventions per 100 steps versus 67.9--70.8, so the remaining gap is not simply caused by intervening more often. The RSS/TTC comparator uses its own interpretable threshold configuration, with soft/hard TTC thresholds of 6.0 s and 3.5 s and a target speed of 20 km/h, rather than an optimized sweep over the proposed framework's threshold. It should therefore be read as an interpretable runtime-filter comparator, not as a tuned upper bound on rule-based filtering. The corresponding bar-chart visualization is retained in the supplementary package.

In this implementation and diagnostic setting, the PPO-Lagrangian row did not recover a progress-preserving low-cost behavior. Its cost limit, Lagrangian initialization, and update settings are reported in Appendix~\ref{app_manifest}; the evaluated policy was not stationary, with 22.1\% route completion and 39.4 km/h mean speed, but it failed through collision and out-road events. The RSS/TTC and PPO-Lagrangian manifests are retained as a baseline audit so that these rows are not read as undocumented cherry-picked failures. We therefore treat the PPO-Lagrangian result as a protocol-specific constrained-baseline outcome rather than as a general statement about training-time constrained RL.

