\subsection{Intervention burden and operating envelope}\label{sec_intervention_burden}
The runtime variants intervene in a large fraction of steps: approximately 68--71 interventions per 100 steps at density 0.15 and more than 80 interventions per 100 steps for the best low-density rows. This should not be interpreted as an ideal deployment property. It indicates that the learned backbone frequently proposes acceleration commands that the monitor flags as high risk under dense traffic. The present contribution is therefore runtime feedback mitigation and diagnostic exposure, not an autonomous policy that has internalized the shield.

The operating envelope is likewise bounded. Density stress shows that the runtime family remains useful at moderate density but degrades under the highest tested density. Sensitivity sweeps show that TTC threshold and target speed alter the balance among cost, route completion, and intervention frequency. Appendix~\ref{app_failure_rollouts} provides one qualitative rollout trace for failure-mode interpretation, with additional high-density traces retained in the supplementary package. These results argue for future training that reduces intervention burden by helping the learned policy internalize guard-compatible behavior, rather than treating high intervention frequency as a finished control solution.

\subsection{Limitations and threats to validity}\label{sec_limitations}
The main threats to validity are bounded but important. Internal validity is limited by the Guard/Shield training-context confound: they share the execution-side operator but are not a strict same-policy ablation. The same-checkpoint runtime ablation in Table~\ref{tab_clean_runtime_ablation} mitigates this concern for the execution operator, but it does not make the separately trained Guard, Shield, and Gated-risk policies into a strict single-policy ranking. Construct validity is limited by heuristic TTC thresholds and by simulator definitions of success, cost, low-progress, and TTC danger. External validity is limited because all evidence comes from MetaDrive simulation rather than real vehicles or hardware-in-the-loop control. Statistical validity is limited by three independently trained seeds; Appendix~\ref{app_ci} reports episode-level intervals, while full seed ranges remain in the supplementary package. Runtime validity is limited because wall-clock measurements are Python simulation-loop timings rather than embedded real-time guarantees. The tested PPO-Lagrangian result is a baseline under this implementation and protocol, not a claim that all constrained RL methods fail. The step-level rollout visualization is a qualitative single-scenario diagnostic and should be read as a failure-mode illustration rather than aggregate evidence.

These limits also define the next research steps. Larger training-seed replication, richer traffic and map scenarios, a broader constrained-RL tuning sweep, and hardware-in-the-loop timing would strengthen the evidence boundary. High intervention frequency suggests a specific opportunity for future work: training the policy to internalize guard-compatible behavior and reduce reliance on execution-time correction.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec_conclusion}

This paper studied progress-preserving safe reinforcement learning in dense simulated traffic as a closed-loop feedback problem. The main finding is that cost-only evaluation can be misleading: a Risk-only policy can achieve zero measured cost by suppressing motion, rather than by regulating risk while completing the route. GuardShield-Runtime addresses this artifact by coupling a learned policy with runtime risk monitoring, bounded acceleration intervention, and progress-safety diagnostics. Across MetaDrive evaluations, runtime-intervention variants reduced cost while preserving route progress, and a same-checkpoint ablation showed that TTC and speed feedback are complementary rather than redundant. The evidence remains bounded to simulation and does not establish formal safety certification or real-world vehicle validation. The broader implication is that safe reinforcement learning for dense traffic should be evaluated as monitored feedback regulation, where cost, progress, intervention burden, and closed-loop behavior are interpreted jointly.

